<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- NO App Sandbox, and that is the whole point of this file now.

	     /n/macos and /n/home are meant to be the Mac's filesystem and the
	     user's home directory -- that is the feature, not a placeholder. A
	     sandboxed app cannot serve either: measured on 2026-08-15, the SAME
	     netfs server binary exporting /Users/christie works from a plain
	     process and hangs the guest from inside the sandbox, while a granted
	     subfolder (~/Makan) works in both. The security-scoped bookmark is
	     enough to mount and not enough to enumerate, so the guest blocks on a
	     reply that never comes.

	     So the Mac app is Developer ID-signed and notarised rather than
	     sandboxed and App Store-distributed. That is a distribution decision
	     with a real cost -- no Mac App Store -- taken deliberately, because a
	     share of a folder the user had to nominate is not the thing that was
	     asked for. The iPad app is unaffected: it keeps its own entitlements
	     and there is no ~ to share there anyway.

	     Hardened Runtime stays on (ENABLE_HARDENED_RUNTIME=YES in the
	     project); notarisation requires it. Nothing else is needed: outside
	     the sandbox the network and file entitlements that used to be here
	     are inert, and an app may always talk to its own loopback, which is
	     all SIMH, the 5620 and netfs ever do. -->
</dict>
</plist>
